{"id":"4a3908e2-da4e-4ff5-b70d-df136ebd3586","object":"chat.completion","created":1785035407,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n        else:\\n            children_map.setdefault(n['parent'], []).append(nid)\\n    # Compute subtree symbol counts for each node (only needed for removal check)\\n    # We'll compute on the fly or precompute? Precompute to avoid repeated recursion.\\n    # Since we may remove nodes, we need dynamic counts. But we process in depth order,\\n    # and when a node is removed, its entire subtree goes away. So we can compute original\\n    # subtree counts, then during processing, maintain a set of removed ids and adjust.\\n    # Simpler: for each candidate, check if removal would cause loss of required symbols\\n    # by scanning the current retained nodes. But that's O(n^2). Since n is small, it's fine.\\n    # We'll maintain a set of retained ids.\\n    retained_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    removed_order = []\\n    # Sort nodes: depth descending, then id ascending (string).\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained_ids:\\n            continue  # already removed (as descendant of a previous removal)\\n        # Cannot remove root\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Cannot remove if it has retained children\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for cid in children_map.get(nid, []):\\n            if cid in retained_ids:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if all required symbols remain after removal of this subtree\\n        # Get set of nodes in subtree (include n itself and all descendants)\\n        # We'll collect subtree ids via DFS\\n        subtree_ids = set()\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            subtree_ids.add(cur)\\n            for cid in children_map.get(cur, []):\\n                if cid in retained_ids:\\n                    stack.append(cid)\\n        # Compute current symbols from retained nodes\\n        current_symbols = {}\\n        for rid in retained_ids:\\n            sym = node_map[rid]['symbol']\\n            current_symbols[sym] = current_symbols.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        # Compute symbols in subtree\\n        subtree_symbols = {}\\n        for sid in subtree_ids:\\n            sym = node_map[sid]['symbol']\\n            subtree_symbols[sym] = subtree_symbols.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        # After removal, new counts\\n        new_symbols = {}\\n        for sym, cnt in current_symbols.items():\\n            sub_cnt = subtree_symbols.get(sym, 0)\\n            if cnt - sub_cnt > 0:\\n                new_symbols[sym] = cnt - sub_cnt\\n        # Check all required symbols present\\n        feasible = True\\n        for req in required_symbols:\\n            if new_symbols.get(req, 0) < 1:\\n                feasible = False\\n                break\\n        if not feasible:\\n            continue\\n        # Remove the subtree\\n        for sid in subtree_ids:\\n            retained_ids.discard(sid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    # Retained nodes are those still in retained_ids\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained_ids)\\n    # Return exactly the required keys\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that returns removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids according to the task description. The case payload has nodes (list of objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols. The objective: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" similar to Perses syntax-guided reduction. We need to consider deeper nodes first, never remove root, required symbols, or nodes with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order (removed_node_ids in order of removal? The output schema says removed_node_ids array, but not explicitly sorted. The candidate artifact says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in order of removal, retained_node_ids sorted. Also need to ensure that we don't mutate case. We need to process nodes in order: deeper first, then by depth? Actually \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, and within same depth, by ID (stable tie break). For each node, if removable and not root and not in required_symbols (but required_symbols are symbols, not IDs; we need to check if the node's symbol is required? Actually \"Never remove a node with a required symbol\"? The description says \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" And \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So required_symbols is a list of symbols that must be present in the tree after removal. So we need to track which symbols are present. Approach:\n- Build a tree structure from nodes: map id->node, and also children mapping.\n- Identify root (parent null).\n- For each node, if its symbol is in required_symbols, it must remain (unless there is another node with same symbol? The requirement says \"all required symbols remain represented\". That means after removal, there should be at least one node with that symbol. So if a node has a required symbol, we might be able to remove it if another node with same symbol exists. However, the candidate artifact says \"Never remove ... a node with a required symbol\"? That seems contradictory. The earlier description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It says \"required symbol\" not \"node with a required symbol\". But required_symbols is a list of symbols, not nodes. It likely means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols should not be removed? But then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies we can remove a node with a required symbol if another node with that symbol remains. Let's parse carefully.\n\nThe task description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"required symbol\" could be interpreted as \"a node that is the only representative of a required symbol\"? But that's ambiguous. Let's look at the candidate artifact: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" And \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree. So a node with a required symbol can be removed if its symbol is still present elsewhere. However, the phrase \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be a simplification meaning you cannot remove the only node providing that symbol. I think the correct interpretation is: you cannot remove a node if that would cause a required symbol to disappear from the tree. So we check that condition.\n\nAlso \"never remove ... a node with retained children\". If a node has children that are not removed, you cannot remove it because that would orphan its children? In a tree, if you remove a node, its children become children of its parent? Or they are deleted? In Perses reduction, removing a node means deleting that node and its entire subtree (or just the node and reattach children?). Usually in syntax-guided reduction, removing a node means deleting that node and its entire subtree. But the description says \"never remove a node with retained children\". That suggests if a node has children that we decide to keep (retain), we cannot remove the node because that would also remove those children. So we must only consider removal of leaf nodes or nodes whose entire subtree is being removed? This is a typical greedy reduction: we try to remove a node, and if after removal the tree is still \"interesting\" (preserves required symbols and grammar valid?), then we remove it. But here we don't have a grammar validity check beyond required symbols? The candidate artifact mentions \"grammar_valid\", but the case payload does not provide grammar. So perhaps we only need to consider the required_symbols condition and the structural constraints (root, removable, children retained). The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That's the interestingness oracle: required symbols must remain. Also \"grammar_valid\" might be automatically satisfied by the tree structure? But we don't have grammar rules. I think the task is simplified: we only have required_symbols as the oracle. Also \"registered_candidate_order\" refers to processing deeper first, tie break by ID. \"stable_ties\" means use ID as tie break.\n\nAlso \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID (string comparison? Probably lexicographic). For removal order, we process in that order, and when we remove a node, we adjust the tree (remove that node and its subtree). But the problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order we removed them.\n\nNow, the algorithm:\n- Build a dict of nodes by id.\n- Build parent-child relationships: for each node, if parent is not null, add node id to child list of parent.\n- Also track which nodes are retained initially (all).\n- Initialize removed_order = [].\n- Process nodes in sorted order: first by depth descending, then by id ascending (or using ID as tie break; we need to specify string compare).\n- For each node in that order:\n   - Check if node is root (parent null) -> skip.\n   - Check if node.removable is False -> skip.\n   - Check if the node has any children that are still retained (i.e., not removed) -> skip because removing it would also remove retained children (since subtree deletion). Actually if we remove a node, we would remove all its descendant nodes. So if any descendant is retained, we cannot remove it because it would remove that descendant. But we are processing deeper first, so children might already be removed? Actually deeper nodes are processed before shallower nodes. So when we process a node, its descendants (deeper) have already been considered and possibly removed. So if a node has children that were not removed (retained), then we cannot remove the node because that would remove those retained children. So we check if any child is still retained (i.e., not in removed_order). If yes, skip.\n   - Then tentatively check if removing this node (and its entire subtree) would cause any required symbol to be lost. But if we remove the node, we lose all symbols in the subtree. So we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node outside this subtree (including ancestors and other branches) that has that symbol. So we can compute current counts of symbols in the tree, and for the node's subtree, we compute the symbol counts within. Then after removal, the new counts = current counts - subtree counts. Check if for each required symbol, new count >= 1. If yes, then we can remove.\n   - If removable, add node.id to removed_order, and also remove all descendants of that node from the tree (mark as removed). Since we already processed descendants, they might already be removed, but we need to ensure we don't process them again. We can simply mark the node and all its descendants as removed (e.g., add to a set of removed ids). But our iteration is based on original order; we can skip nodes already removed.\n- After processing, retained_node_ids = all nodes not in removed_order, sorted (by id? The output says \"retained_node_ids\" : array of strings, but not explicit sorting. The candidate says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So we sort them lexicographically? Use id string. Also removed_node_ids in removal order.\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(remaining_ids)}.\n\nEdge cases: root may not be in required_symbols? root is \"root\" symbol; required_symbols may include \"root\". We never remove root anyway. Also, if a required symbol appears only in a subtree that we remove, we cannot remove that subtree. So we need to check.\n\nWe need to implement this in Python. The solve function takes case (the payload object). It should not mutate case. We'll copy nodes list if needed.\n\nAlso note: the case payload may have _case_nonce; we ignore it.\n\nLet's write the code. We'll also include a check for validity: raise ValueError if infeasible? Not specified but maybe if required_symbols not satisfied initially? But it should be initially satisfied. We'll just compute.\n\nWe need to output the implementation as a JSON string within { \"implementation\": \"...\" }.\n\nMake sure to escape quotes and newlines.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":2920,"total_tokens":5092,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2002},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}